To show this in detail, we will have to study various extensions of Q(α), corresponding precisely to
adjoining roots of elements which fail to be square in the ring of integ ers. In particular, the standard bounds
on the discriminant of Q(α) extends to similar bounds on the discriminant of the quadratic exte nsions of
interest, and we use eﬀective results of Stark [54] to show that th e majority of such extensions have no
Siegel zero. This allows us to show that for characters of suitably la rge conductor, the kernel of Ψ F is small
enough that it can be handled by brute force.
