\[
\Pr(\ker\chi\text{ contains a nonexceptional nonzero class})
\le |H_S|(1-1/(4d))^J\le N^{-2}.
\]

Hence the surviving square-class kernel has dimension at most one, uniformly over every admissible field. LV’s random-field Lemma 6.10 is unnecessary here.

### Linear algebra and actual square roots [proved/cited]

The matrix has at most the stated column count and \(O(m\log N)\) nonzero entries. Use [Kaltofen–Saunders (1991), Theorems 2–4 and §2](https://kaltofen.math.ncsu.edu/bibliography/91/KaSa91.pdf), the rigorous singular-system Wiedemann method, over \(\mathbb F_{2^w}\) of cardinality at least \(m^4\). Zero-padding handles rectangular matrices; Toeplitz preconditioning preserves fast multiplication. Their rank error is \(O(m^2/2^w)\), and the cost is \(O(m)\) matrix applications plus \(\widetilde O(m^2)\) field operations. Expand extension-field solutions in an \(\mathbb F_2\)-basis, retaining two independent binary kernel vectors; verify them and retry on failure. Their uniform-solution theorem gives constant expected retries. Bit cost is \(m^{2+o(1)}\).

For two independent dependencies \(s_1,s_2\), at least one of \(s_1,s_2,s_1+s_2\) has trivial algebraic square class. Test these three directly. This avoids searching a class group or guessing that quadratic characters have eliminated every obstruction.

Here is a rigorous square-root algorithm, independent of Montgomery/Couveignes/Thomé implementation claims. For a tested product \(\Gamma\), choose integer \(D_0\), a product of norms of inverted atomic elements, clearing its denominators. Then \(Z=D_0^2\Gamma\in A\). If \(\eta^2=Z\), then \(\eta\in O_K\) and \(\delta\eta\in A\). The latter follows from the trace-dual identity \(A^*=\delta^{-1}A\) and \(O_K\subset A^*\).

The coefficients of \(U=\delta^2Z\), and of any integral \(w\) with \(w^2=U\), have \(T=O(m\log N)\) bits. Explicit bounds follow from Cauchy’s root bound for \(Q\), the nonzero integer norms of each \(z_P\), and inversion of the embedding Vandermonde matrix, whose determinant has absolute value \(\sqrt{|D|}\ge1\). All degree-dependent constants are fixed.

At any sampled prime, \(Q\) is squarefree and \(U\) is a unit. Factor the finite algebra, enumerate its at most \(2^d\) square roots, and Newton–Hensel lift each via \(w\mapsto(w+U/w)/2\), doubling precision until the modulus exceeds twice the coefficient bound. Center the coefficients and verify \(w^2=U\) exactly. A global square supplies one of these branches; no verified branch means nonsquare. Balanced products and fast integer arithmetic cost \(\widetilde O_d(T)(\log N)^{O(1)}\subset m^{1+o(1)}\).

For a verified branch,

\[
\psi(\sqrt\Gamma)=\frac{w(tg_0/g_1)}{D_0\,Q'(tg_0/g_1)}\pmod N.
\]

These denominators are units: \(D_0\) uses only \(t\) and smooth norms, and \(Q'(tg_0/g_1)=t^{d-2}f'(g_0/g_1)\). Compute the rational square root of \(\prod q_i\) by halving its recorded exponents, using modular inverses for negative exponents. This is **not** square-root extraction modulo an unfactored \(N\). Multiplying it by the displayed algebraic image gives \(Y\); \(X=\prod r_iW_i\). Both are units.

Every operation above, including retries and branch selection, uses invariant certificates and independent coins. Choose the first verified dependency in a fixed order before examining any hidden root or final gcd. This proves \(T\). After a trivial final gcd, restart with fresh hidden roots, as DD requires.

### Exceptional events and field law

**[Proved bounds.]** Let \(\mu=\mathbb E q_A=L^{-o(1)}\). A field event of original probability \(\epsilon\) has kept probability at most \(\epsilon/\mu\).

| Event | Bound before / after keeping; treatment |
|---|---|
| \(g_1\) nonunit | Proper factor immediately; \(0<g_1<N\), so no whole-\(N\) failure. |
| Degree drop | Zero after the preceding check: \(t\equiv a_d\not\equiv0\pmod{g_1}\). |
| Reducible \(f\) | \(O_d(H^{-1/3}\log H)\) / \(N^{-h/3+o(1)}\); detect and reject. |
| \(t\) or \(f'(g_0/g_1)\) nonunit | Proper gcd is success; whole-\(N\) rejection has probability at most \(2/N+2/(2H+1)\), divided by \(\mu\) after keeping. |
| Zero discriminant | Impossible for an irreducible characteristic-zero polynomial. |
| Index, bad primes, units, 2-torsion | Arbitrary occurrence probability; included in \(H_S\), with no rejection. |
| Exceptional zero | Arbitrary occurrence probability; at most one nonzero class, handled by three tests. |
| Character miss | At most \(N^{-2}\), conditional on any field; resample characters if no square verifies. |
| Solver/finite-field randomization | Verified outputs; constant expected retries, zero incorrect-output probability. |
| Final trivial gcd | Exactly \(2^{1-\omega(N)}\); fresh-root restart. |

For completeness, the reducibility estimate needs no unproved transfer. Fix \(g\), omit primes dividing \(g_1N\), and take primes \(p\asymp H^{1/3}\). Reduction of the coefficient box modulo \(p\), or \(pq\), differs from uniform by \(O_d(p/H)\), or \(O_d(pq/H)\). The affine family modulo \(p\) consists of all degree-at-most-\(d\) polynomials with a prescribed nonzero value. A fraction bounded below by \(1/(2d)\) are degree-\(d\) irreducibles: count norm fibres in \(\mathbb F_{p^d}^\times\), discard proper subfields, and divide by \(d\), summing over nonzero leading coefficients. The variance method gives \(O(1/\pi(H^{1/3})+H^{-1/3})\) probability of no irreducible reduction. Every reducible polynomial has no such reduction. The whole-\(N\) bounds follow because \(t\) is affine in \(c_0\), and \(f'(g_0/g_1)\) is affine in the constant coefficient of \(R_c\), each with unit coefficient \(g_1\) modulo \(N\).

**[Proved consequence.]** None of FM, R, Kern or Theorem 4.3 needs alteration. Whole-\(N\) rejections and reducibility have polynomially small probability, negligible even against their \(L^{O(1)}\) maximum candidate counts and after kept-field amplification. Proper gcds terminate successfully. Only assembly bookkeeping and the search implementation change.

## 2. HECM and alternatives

**\(i\) [Cited/proved completion.]** [LPP I, Theorem 1.3, p. 399](https://math.dartmouth.edu/~carlp/hyperI.pdf), checked against the page image, states: with \(c_1=1980000^{1/3}\), effective \(x\ge x_3\), \(z=L_x[2/3,c_1]\), and \(x^{3/5}\le y\le x\), the interval \([x,x+y]\) contains at least

\[
y\exp[-(\log x)^{1/3}(\log\log x)^{8/3}]
\]

\(z\)-smooth integers. Take \(x=p^2-p^{3/2}/2\), \(y=p^{3/2}\). The range holds, and this count is \(p^{3/2}L_p[2/3,-o(1)]\gg p\). [LPP II, Theorem 1.1](https://math.dartmouth.edu/~carlp/PDF/paper129.pdf) applies for every prime \(p>8100\), with at most \(p\) exceptional orders and the stated quintic fibre bound.

Insert this into genus2rh Proposition B. Propositions A/B and Lemmas 1–4 give success probability \(L_p[2/3,-o(1)]/(\log n)^{O(1)}\) per curve; work per curve is \(L_p[2/3,2^{2/3}c_1+o(1)](\log n)^{O(1)}\). Doubling the guess for \(p\), with reciprocal-success trial counts, has successive cost ratio tending to one and a geometric failure tail, exactly as genus2rh §4. Bounded primes are handled by trial division. This supplies the bookkeeping left as a sketch in §6.3, unconditionally, with a weaker polylog factor than LPP’s advertised bound.

Recursively split, certify primes and detect perfect powers. For every \(B\)-smooth \(n\le N^{O(1)}\), all recursive least primes are at most \(B\); there are \(O(\log N)\) splits. Expected work is \(L_B[2/3,O(1)](\log N)^{O(1)}=L^{o(1)}\). Markov truncation at twice this uniform bound gives recognition probability at least \(1/2\), with no false smooth declarations. Thus the requested test follows without citing an unpublished Part III as a theorem.

**\(ii\) [Proved alternative.]** [Bernstein, Algorithm 2.1 and Theorems 2.2–2.3](https://nymity.ch/anomalous-tor-keys/bibliography/pdf/Bernstein04.pdf) compute smooth parts in \(\widetilde O(b)\) time, where \(b\) is total input bits. Enumerate the entire capped special-\(q\) list before testing; it contains \(O(S^2)\) candidates, independently of test outcomes. Batch both integers for all candidates and return the first successful candidate in the original order. The deterministic cap becomes \((B+S^2)L^{o(1)}\). Factor the returned integers by trial division in \(BL^{o(1)}\), also within this cap.

At A’s optimum, \(a=\theta/\sqrt2\) and \(S^2=L^{1/\sqrt2+o(1)}\ge B\), since \(\theta<1\). Thus the exponent is unchanged. Adaptivity between searches or fields is irrelevant: each individual capped list is a batch. Individual Pollard–Strassen/Costa–Harvey tests instead incur A′’s \(B^{1/2+o(1)}\) factor; they do not automatically remove that penalty.

**\(iii\) [Cited/assessment.]** [Lenstra–Pomerance 1992, Algorithm 7.2, Theorem 7.3](https://math.dartmouth.edu/~carlp/PDF/paper85.pdf) recognize numbers supported on their specified good-prime set in \(O((\log n+1)^4\exp(2(\log B)^{6/7}))=L^{o(1)}\). Theorem 7.4 and Corollary 7.5 bound excluded primes, and §6/Theorem 8.1 establish enough recognizable values for their sampler. This does not guarantee recognition of **every** smooth input. Substitution here would require FM for recognizable values under the actual moving progression weights. R and LD’s upper bounds restrict by positivity; Kern is unchanged, but the first-moment lower bound needs additional work.

**\(iv\) [Cited.]** That 1992 shape test uses the foregoing rigorous ECM machinery, not HECM; §7 explicitly derives its guarantees from Lenstra 1987 and Pomerance 1987. It has no unpublished-Part-III dependency.

**\(v\) [Assessment, following proved reductions.]** Retain A’s \(0.9192\ldots\) infimum at \(d=9\), conditional on LD and the repository’s other proved inputs. It is an infimum over fixed \(k\), not an attained finite-\(k\) constant. A′’s \(0.9670\ldots\) is an available individual-test fallback. LV6 itself needs neither HECM nor smoothness testing of new integers.

**UNVERIFIED.** LD remains open. I did not independently verify Pomerance 1987 Theorem 2.1 from its scanned original; its relevant guarantees were checked in LP 1992 §§6–8. I make no claim that the original Montgomery/Couveignes/Thomé implementations have the required uniform complexity; the explicit fixed-degree Hensel construction above replaces that question. No unpublished LPP III was located or used.

