+# LV6 at fixed degree, and the smoothness-test caveat
+
+**Verdict [proved below, using cited theorems].** Fixed odd degree permits an unconditional LV6 adaptation, uniformly over suitable fields. One potentially exceptional square class can be retained and eliminated by testing three dependencies. HECM is unnecessary for the headline: batching each complete capped search preserves Theorem A’s exponent. The conclusion remains **conditional on LD**: at \(d=9\), the infimum constant is \(0.9192\ldots\), not the more expensive A′ constant.
+
+Throughout, \(L=L_N[1/2,1]\), \(d,k,h,a>0\) are fixed as specified in the question, and \(m=B L^{o(1)}\) denotes the number of rows. Polynomial factors in \(\log N\) are \(m^{o(1)}\). Labels distinguish proofs here from references and assessments.
+
+## 1. Proposition LV6
+
+**Proposition [proved].** Let \(N\) be odd, composite, not a prime power, with no prime divisor at most \(B=L^a\). Let \(f\) be irreducible of fixed odd degree \(d\), with coefficients of size \(N^{O(1)}\); put
+
+\[
+t=f_d\ne0,\quad K=\mathbb Q(\alpha),\quad\beta=t\alpha,\quad
+Q(T)=t^{d-1}f(T/t),\quad A=\mathbb Z[\beta],\quad\delta=Q'(\beta).
+\]
+
+Suppose \(F(g_0,g_1)=N\), and \(g_1,t,f'(g_0/g_1)\) are units modulo \(N\). Given certified pure descent rows, each containing at most \(2k\) atomic relations of the stated height and smoothness, there is a Las Vegas algorithm producing a **nonzero row dependency** and \(X^2=Y^2\pmod N\) in expected \(m^{2+o(1)}\) bit operations, provided
+
+\[
+m\ge (d+1)\pi(B)+J+4,\qquad J=C\log N
+\]
+
+for a sufficiently large effective constant \(C\). Both the dependency and \(Y\) depend only on the invariant transcript and independent coins. Consequently DD gives fruitfulness exactly \(1-2^{1-\omega(N)}\), assuming fresh independent hidden-root draws as in DD. The surplus condition is necessary; merely specifying an asymptotic row-count scale does not guarantee a kernel.
+
+Here \(\|f\|_\infty=O_d(MH)=N^{O(1)}\), and \(|a|,b\le V=N^{1/(4k)}L^{O(1)}\). Height enters through polynomial discriminant bounds, \(O(\log N)\) bit lengths per atom, and \(O(m\log N)\) bit lengths per product. No postprocessing step enumerates a box of side \(V\). LV Theorem 2.6, which assumes growing degree and \(L[1/3]\) pair heights, is not invoked outside its hypotheses; the proof below replaces that specialization.
+
+### Certificates and parity [proved]
+
+For an atomic relation write \(G(P)=\ell n_P\), with signed smooth \(n_P\), and set \(z_P=ta-b\beta\). Then
+
+\[
+\psi(z_P)=t\ell n_P/g_1,\qquad
+\operatorname{Norm}(z_P)=t^{d-1}F(a,b).
+\]
+
+Substitution into \(u_i/v_i\) gives, with signed multiplicities \(e_{iP}\),
+
+\[
+r_i^2=q_i\psi(\Gamma_i),\quad
+\Gamma_i=\prod_Pz_P^{e_{iP}},\quad
+q_i=\prod_P(g_1/(t n_P))^{e_{iP}}.
+\]
+
+Known invariant square multipliers, if present, are incorporated in DD’s \(W_i\). Record the rational sign, all rational prime exponents up to \(B\), and \(E_i=\sum_Pe_{iP}\pmod2\). This last column handles **both** \(g_1\) and \(t\): their exponents are \(E_i,-E_i\). Modulo two it is the number of atomic relations, counted with multiplicity. Factoring \(t\) is unnecessary.
+
+Put \(D=\operatorname{disc}Q\), and let \(S\) be the prime ideals above rational primes dividing \(2tD\). For each \(p\le B\) with \(p\nmid tD\), record the valuations belonging to every simple root of \(Q\bmod p\), including zero. A primitive linear element can vanish only at its corresponding degree-one prime; its valuation is \(v_p(F(a,b))\). If \(p\mid b\), its norm is a unit at \(p\). Thus these columns are computable from the supplied factorizations. Bad primes are deliberately left in \(S\).
+
+Every resulting algebraic kernel element belongs to
+
+\[
+H_S=\{x\in K^\times:v_{\mathfrak p}(x)\equiv0\ (2)
+\text{ for }\mathfrak p\notin S\}/K^{\times2}.
+\]
+
+This treats nonintegral \(\alpha\) by the monic order \(A\). In particular, \([O_K:A]^2\mid D\); no maximal-order computation is required. The exact identity is \(D=t^{(d-1)(d-2)}\operatorname{disc}f\), so \(\log|D|=O(\log N)\).
+
+### Residual classes and characters [proved, with exact citations]
+
+The valuation map at \(S\), followed by the usual ideal-square map, bounds
+
+\[
+|H_S|\le2^{|S|+d}|\operatorname{Cl}(K)[2]|
+\le2^{|S|+d}h_K=N^{O(1)}.
+\]
+
+Indeed, its everywhere-even kernel is an extension of a subgroup of class-group 2-torsion by units modulo squares; Dirichlet gives at most \(2^d\) unit classes. Here \(|S|=O(\log N)\), and Minkowski’s ideal bound gives \(h_K\le |D_K|^{1/2}(\log|D_K|)^{O_d(1)}\), as also derived in [LV, Claim 6.5](https://arxiv.org/pdf/1805.08873). Units and 2-torsion are bounded, never assumed trivial.
+
+For each nonzero \(h\in H_S\), let \(L_h=K(\sqrt h)\). At odd primes outside \(S\) this extension is unramified. At odd primes in \(S\) its relative discriminant exponent is at most one; at dyadic primes it is at most \(2v_{\mathfrak p}(2)+1\), by removing square factors and using the polynomial discriminant \(4h\) locally. Consequently
+
+\[
+|D_{L_h}|\le |D_K|^2\,2^{3d}|tD|^d=N^{O(1)}.
+\]
+
+This avoids needing small element representatives for unit classes.
+
+Use [Lagarias–Odlyzko, Theorem 1.3, (1.6)–(1.7)](https://aareyanmanzoor.github.io/assets/articles/lagarias-odlyzko.pdf), with \([L_h:\mathbb Q]=2d\) and \(\log x=(\log N)^4\). Its condition \(\log x\ge10(2d)(\log|D_{L_h}|)^2\) holds. Unless an exceptional zero has \((1-\beta_0)^{-1}>(\log N)^2\), its exceptional term and error are \(o(x/\log x)\), uniformly in \(h\).
+
+There is **at most one** such exceptional nonzero class. [Stark, Lemma 8, p. 145](https://gdz.sub.uni-goettingen.de/download/pdf/PPN356556735_0023/PPN356556735_0023.pdf) transfers that zero to a quadratic subfield \(F\subset L_h\): the required threshold \(4(2d)!\log|D_{L_h}|\) is \(O_d(\log N)\). Since \(d\) is odd, \(K\) has no quadratic subfield and \(L_h=KF\). Two quadratic subfields would generate a degree-four subfield of a degree-\(2d\) field, which is impossible. Different classes therefore give different \(F\). Their discriminants are \(N^{O(1)}\), so Landau–Page uniqueness ([LV, Fact 6.24](https://arxiv.org/pdf/1805.08873)) permits only one with a zero this close to one. No Siegel lower bound is used.
+
+Sample rational primes \(p\in[x,2x]\) uniformly by rejection and deterministic primality certification. Factor \(Q\bmod p\); choose uniformly one of \(d\) slots, populated by its distinct linear factors and padded by trivial characters. Each actual root \(s\) gives
+
+\[
+\chi_{p,s}(z_P)=\left(\frac{ta-bs}{p}\right).
+\]
+
+Each degree-one prime ideal has equal sampling mass. Prime ideals of higher degree contribute only \(O_d(\sqrt x)\); Chebotarev therefore gives detection probability at least \(1/(4d)\) for every nonexceptional nonzero class. All \(p\) exceed the atomic norms and \(2tD\), so these evaluations are nonzero. Sampling, finite-field factoring and evaluation cost \((\log N)^{O_d(1)}\): these primes have polynomially many **bits**.
+
+With \(J=C\log N\), a union bound gives
+
+\[
+\Pr(\ker\chi\text{ contains a nonexceptional nonzero class})
+\le |H_S|(1-1/(4d))^J\le N^{-2}.
+\]
+
+Hence the surviving square-class kernel has dimension at most one, uniformly over every admissible field. LV’s random-field Lemma 6.10 is unnecessary here.
+
