+
+**[Proved consequence.]** None of FM, R, Kern or Theorem 4.3 needs alteration. Whole-\(N\) rejections and reducibility have polynomially small probability, negligible even against their \(L^{O(1)}\) maximum candidate counts and after kept-field amplification. Proper gcds terminate successfully. Only assembly bookkeeping and the search implementation change.
+
+## 2. HECM and alternatives
+
+**(i) [Cited/proved completion.]** [LPP I, Theorem 1.3, p. 399](https://math.dartmouth.edu/~carlp/hyperI.pdf), checked against the page image, states: with \(c_1=1980000^{1/3}\), effective \(x\ge x_3\), \(z=L_x[2/3,c_1]\), and \(x^{3/5}\le y\le x\), the interval \([x,x+y]\) contains at least
+
+\[
+y\exp[-(\log x)^{1/3}(\log\log x)^{8/3}]
+\]
+
+\(z\)-smooth integers. Take \(x=p^2-p^{3/2}/2\), \(y=p^{3/2}\). The range holds, and this count is \(p^{3/2}L_p[2/3,-o(1)]\gg p\). [LPP II, Theorem 1.1](https://math.dartmouth.edu/~carlp/PDF/paper129.pdf) applies for every prime \(p>8100\), with at most \(p\) exceptional orders and quintic fibre lower bound \(p^{9/2}/(24000\lambda(p))\), where \(\lambda(p)=(\log p)^2(\log\log p)^2\).
+
+Genus2rh’s sampling and separation proof gives, for distinct \(p,q\mid n\),
+
+with \(S_w(p)=\#\{z\in[p^2-p^{3/2}/2,p^2+p^{3/2}/2]:P^+(z)\le w\}\),
+
+\[
+\Pr(\text{factor per curve})\ge
+\frac{(S_w(p)-p)(1-4/\sqrt p)}
+{24000\cdot144000\,p^{3/2}\lambda(p)\lambda(q)}.
+\]
+
+This uses only lower fibre bounds. Supply at \(q\) counts group orders prime to the largest prime in the order of the sampled divisor at \(p\). At its killing step, absence of a proper nonunit would force the preceding divisor to be nonzero modulo \(p\) and zero modulo \(q\), contradicting agreement of degrees in Cantor’s algorithm. Thus Propositions A/B and Lemmas 1–4 supply the missing algorithmic separation.
+
+Choose \(w=L_{v^2}[2/3,c_1]\) for a guess \(v\ge p\). Work per curve is \(L_v[2/3,2^{2/3}c_1+o(1)](\log n)^{O(1)}\); reciprocal success probability is \(L_p[2/3,o(1)](\log n)^{O(1)}\). Double \(v\), allocating trials proportional to
+\((\log n)^6\exp((\log(v^2))^{1/3}(\log\log(v^2))^{8/3})\).
+From the first adequate stage, failure probability is at most \(e^{-1}\), while successive stage-cost ratios tend to one. Summing the geometric tail proves the expected bound; bounded primes use fixed trial division. This completes §6.3’s bookkeeping, unconditionally, with a weaker polylog factor than LPP advertised.
+
+Recursively split, certify primes and detect perfect powers. For every \(B\)-smooth \(n\le N^{O(1)}\), all recursive least primes are at most \(B\); there are \(O(\log N)\) splits. Expected work is \(L_B[2/3,O(1)](\log N)^{O(1)}=L^{o(1)}\). Markov truncation at twice this uniform bound gives recognition probability at least \(1/2\), with no false smooth declarations. Thus the requested test follows without citing an unpublished Part III as a theorem.
+
+**(ii) [Proved alternative.]** [Bernstein, Algorithm 2.1 and Theorems 2.2–2.3](https://nymity.ch/anomalous-tor-keys/bibliography/pdf/Bernstein04.pdf) compute smooth parts in \(\widetilde O(b)\) time, where \(b\) is total input bits. Enumerate the entire capped special-\(q\) list before testing; it contains \(O(S^2)\) candidates, independently of test outcomes. Batch both integers for all candidates and return the first successful candidate in the original order. The deterministic cap becomes \((B+S^2)L^{o(1)}\). Factor the returned integers by trial division in \(BL^{o(1)}\), also within this cap.
+
+At A’s optimum, \(a=\theta/\sqrt2\) and \(S^2=L^{1/\sqrt2+o(1)}\ge B\), since \(\theta<1\). Thus the exponent is unchanged. Adaptivity between searches or fields is irrelevant: each individual capped list is a batch. Individual Pollard–Strassen/Costa–Harvey tests instead incur A′’s \(B^{1/2+o(1)}\) factor; they do not automatically remove that penalty.
+
+**(iii) [Cited/assessment.]** [Lenstra–Pomerance 1992, Algorithm 7.2, Theorem 7.3](https://math.dartmouth.edu/~carlp/PDF/paper85.pdf) recognize numbers supported on their specified good-prime set in \(O((\log n+1)^4\exp(2(\log B)^{6/7}))=L^{o(1)}\). Theorem 7.4 and Corollary 7.5 bound excluded primes, and §6/Theorem 8.1 establish enough recognizable values for their sampler. This does not guarantee recognition of **every** smooth input. Substitution here would require FM for recognizable values under the actual moving progression weights. R and LD’s upper bounds restrict by positivity; Kern is unchanged, but the first-moment lower bound needs additional work.
+
+**(iv) [Cited.]** That 1992 shape test uses the foregoing rigorous ECM machinery, not HECM; §7 explicitly derives its guarantees from Lenstra 1987 and Pomerance 1987. It has no unpublished-Part-III dependency.
+
+**(v) [Assessment, following proved reductions.]** Retain A’s \(0.9192\ldots\) infimum at \(d=9\), conditional on LD and the repository’s other proved inputs. It is an infimum over fixed \(k\), not an attained finite-\(k\) constant. A′’s \(0.9670\ldots\) is an available individual-test fallback. LV6 itself needs neither HECM nor smoothness testing of new integers.
+
+**UNVERIFIED.** LD remains open. I did not independently verify Pomerance 1987 Theorem 2.1 from its scanned original; its relevant guarantees were checked in LP 1992 §§6–8. I make no claim that the original Montgomery/Couveignes/Thomé implementations have the required uniform complexity; the explicit fixed-degree Hensel construction above replaces that question. No unpublished LPP III was located or used.

